---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SITE } from "../site";
---

<Layout
  title="About"
  description="The physics, science-communication and digital-strategy background behind Ryan Laird's development work."
>
  <div class="wrap page">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Dev</a> / About</nav>

    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="sl">About</p>
      <h1>Where the code comes from</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        My work sits where <strong>scientific understanding, strategic communication and practical digital
        building</strong> meet. This page explains how that background shapes what I build.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="do-title">
      <p class="tlbl">What I do</p>
      <h2 id="do-title">A physicist’s habits, applied to software</h2>
      <p>
        I began in physics and astrophysics, studying how complex systems can be observed, modelled and
        explained. That foundation still shapes how I approach technology: ask what the evidence says,
        understand the system before changing it, and make the result understandable to the people who need
        to use it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Over the past decade I have worked across <strong>space innovation, Earth observation, sustainability
        and climate technology</strong>, helping organisations translate technical capability into clear
        stories, services and opportunities. I am a <strong>Chartered Marketer</strong> with experience in
        strategic communications, science communication, service design, events and digital strategy.
      </p>
      <p>
        This developer site is where that background becomes practical: small systems, visualisations and
        utilities that help me test ideas, work with data and communicate more effectively.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="prose-cols">
      <section class="block" aria-labelledby="phys-title">
        <p class="tlbl">Physics and science</p>
        <h2 id="phys-title">Comfortable with uncertainty</h2>
        <p>
          My academic background is in <strong>physics with astrophysics</strong>, including an MPhys (Hons)
          and doctoral research in astronomy. Physics taught me to reason from evidence and to look for the
          underlying structure behind a problem.
        </p>
        <p>Whether I am building a data dashboard, designing an API or exploring a simulation, I ask the same questions:</p>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li>What is actually being measured?</li>
          <li>What assumptions sit behind the model?</li>
          <li>How can complexity be made visible without oversimplifying it?</li>
          <li>How can a tool help someone make a better decision?</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          I am particularly interested in <strong>space, Earth observation, climate and environmental
          data</strong>, where scientific evidence, public understanding and practical action need to connect.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="block" aria-labelledby="sci-title">
        <p class="tlbl">Science communication</p>
        <h2 id="sci-title">Useful only if people can use it</h2>
        <p>
          Science only creates value when people can understand it, trust it and act on it. My
          science-communication work has focused on making technical subjects accessible without losing their
          accuracy, working with space-sector organisations, researchers and mission-driven teams on editorial
          content, events, digital platforms and stakeholder engagement.
        </p>
        <p>I care about communication, and therefore interfaces and documentation, that is:</p>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li><strong>Clear</strong>: plain language, strong structure and honest explanations</li>
          <li><strong>Credible</strong>: grounded in evidence and transparent about uncertainty</li>
          <li><strong>Useful</strong>: designed around what an audience needs to understand or do</li>
          <li><strong>Responsible</strong>: especially where technology affects sustainability, climate or public trust</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Each project here should explain not only what it does, but why it exists and what problem it is trying to solve.</p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="mkt-title">
      <p class="tlbl">Marketing and digital strategy</p>
      <h2 id="mkt-title">Technology is also about audience and adoption</h2>
      <p>
        As a Chartered Marketer, I bring a strategic-communications perspective to the things I build. My
        background includes content strategy, digital campaigns, SEO, stakeholder engagement and event
        delivery across the space, sustainability and technology sectors. In practice that means I ask
        questions beyond the code:
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Who is this for?</li>
        <li>What should they understand after using it?</li>
        <li>What action should it enable?</li>
        <li>How will we know whether it worked?</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn" href="/stack/">See the working stack</a>
        <a class="btn" href={SITE.links.cv} rel="noopener">Full CV ↗</a>
        <a class="btn" href={SITE.links.contact} rel="noopener">Get in touch ↗</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
